                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Interloper is a really interesting little strategy game because it takes the usual real time strategy formula and cuts away almost everything that normally makes an RTS feel complicated. Instead of building a huge base, researching loads of technology and spending ages preparing an army, I am dropped straight into a small map and have to take control of territory before the match ends. The whole thing is built around speed and positioning, with matches designed to last around five minutes. I like that because I can understand what I am doing almost immediately, but there is still enough going on for me to make mistakes and improve.\n\nThe biggest thing I like is that I am not actually trying to destroy an enemy base in the normal way. The goal is to claim the map by controlling territory, which makes movement and predicting what the enemy is going to do much more important. I have to think about where I can expand, where I might get cut off and how I can surround areas before the other side reaches them. It gives the game a very different feeling from a traditional RTS because there is much less focus on building up a giant force and much more focus on making the right move at the right time.\n\nThe short matches also work really well for me. I can play a match in only a few minutes, which makes it easy to start another one without feeling like I am committing to a huge session. At the same time, the simple rules do not mean the game is completely mindless. The more I understand the map and the way the territory system works, the more I start thinking ahead instead of simply reacting to whatever is happening. There is also AI with different difficulty levels and a single player mode with more content, so I am not forced to jump straight into multiplayer if I want to learn the game first.\n\nThe biggest weakness for me is that the simplicity can also make the game feel limited. There are not loads of units, buildings or research options for me to experiment with, so anyone expecting a traditional large scale RTS is probably going to find this much too stripped down. I actually think that is the point, but it still affects how much variety I get from it. Once I understand the main territory system, most of my improvement comes from getting better at positioning and prediction rather than unlocking completely new ways to play.\n\nI also think the presentation is better than it initially looks. The simple two dimensional style makes it easy for me to see the important parts of the map without having lots of visual clutter getting in the way. The soundtrack also fits the quick pace nicely, and the whole game feels focused rather than overloaded with menus and information. There are also achievements, Steam Cloud and leaderboards, which give me a few extra reasons to keep playing and improving. The game is free to play as well, which makes the simple design much easier for me to appreciate.\n\nOverall, Interloper is a very focused strategy game that I think succeeds because it knows exactly what it wants to be. I like the five minute matches, territory based victory condition and the importance of predicting movement. It does not have the huge amount of content or systems that I would normally expect from an RTS, and that limits the long term variety for me, but it also means I can understand the game quickly and start improving without spending hours learning complicated mechanics. For me, it is a clever little strategy game that is much deeper than its simple presentation suggests, and the fact that it is free makes it even easier to recommend.\n\nRating: 7.8/10\n",
